---
/**
 * /find-your-helper — the page that explains what DirectHired actually
 * does, for the visitor who has not yet decided to send anything.
 *
 * Core-pages design spec §3.2 gives it four parts and no others:
 * matching explained · `Process` reframed as "what happens after you
 * submit" · the response expectation · the form CTA. It owns §4's *maid
 * placement Singapore* intent, which the <title> and the description carry
 * once each, inside sentences a person would read.
 *
 * IT IS NOT A HELPER SEARCH, FILTER OR BROWSE. Spec §7 and source brief
 * §40 rule that out for the whole site, and this is the page a contributor
 * would most naturally add one to — its URL says "find your helper", and
 * every competitor puts a gallery of profiles behind that phrase. There is
 * nothing to filter here on purpose: the product IS the matching, and a
 * catalogue would describe a different business. If a future task wants a
 * list of helpers on this page, that is a spec change, not a page change.
 *
 * WHAT THIS PAGE MAY SAY. Every fact below already exists somewhere the
 * client has approved: the four process steps come from Process.astro
 * (single-sourced, reframed by a prop, never copied), the response
 * expectation is DirectHired's own "within 1 business day" as published in
 * src/content/faq/response-time.md, and the two placement timings are spec
 * §2.2's approximately 2 weeks / approximately 1 week. Nothing here rounds,
 * extends or infers past those. Four claims that would have been natural
 * to write are deliberately absent, because nobody has supplied them:
 * how long the interview stage usually takes, how many helpers a
 * recommendation typically contains, what proportion of families accept
 * the first shortlist, and what families ask after seeing how matching
 * works. Each needs DirectHired, not an author.
 *
 * (Fix round, F-6: this comment listed three and named the first, second
 * and third; the task report listed three and named the fourth in place of
 * the third. All four are genuinely absent from the page, so the count
 * here was wrong in the direction that hides an open question. The list is
 * now the union, and the number matches its own list.)
 *
 * Section grounds run cream / teal / white / cream across the four blocks
 * (this page's .helper-match, Process, this page's .match-timing,
 * FinalCta). Process is fixed at --color-surface-teal and FinalCta at
 * --color-surface, which is what fixes the other two; the sequence is
 * registered and asserted in tests/section-rhythm.test.ts. Block 07 of the
 * homepage keeps --color-deep as the site's only palette register shift —
 * TwoSidedMatch is deliberately NOT rendered here, both because spec §3.2
 * does not list it for this page and because a second dark band is a
 * decision this page has no reason to take.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import Container from '../components/Container.astro'
import Button from '../components/Button.astro'
import Process from '../sections/Process.astro'
import FinalCta from '../sections/FinalCta.astro'
import { company } from '../data/company'
import { defaultWhatsappUrl } from '../lib/whatsapp'

const whatsappHref = defaultWhatsappUrl()

/*
 * The two halves a match is made from. Kept as data rather than typed into
 * the markup twice so the two panels cannot drift apart in length or
 * shape — and so that adding a third half is a data edit somebody reviews
 * rather than a copy-paste.
 *
 * Both entries are restatements of facts already on the site: the
 * household side is Process step 1 ("your household, your routines, and
 * the specific tasks, schedule and expectations a helper would take on"),
 * the helper side is the Helper column of TwoSidedMatch (block 07). They
 * are restated rather than imported because this page explains the INPUTS
 * to a match, and block 07 is a composition about the outcome — reusing
 * that section here would put its dark band and its "Happy Employer.
 * Happy Helper." line on a page that spec §3.2 does not give them to.
 */
const matchInputs = [
  {
    title: 'What we ask you about',
    body: 'Your household and how it runs — the tasks a helper would take on, the schedule they would work to, and the expectations you want met. That conversation happens before any name is put in front of you.',
  },
  {
    title: 'What we know about a helper',
    body: 'The work a helper has done before, the responsibilities that experience suits them to, and what they expect from the household they join.',
  },
]

const title = 'Maid Placement in Singapore — How We Match | DirectHired'
const description =
  'Maid placement in Singapore, explained: how DirectHired matches a domestic helper to your ' +
  'household, and what happens after you submit your requirements.'
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} breadcrumb="Find Your Helper">
  <section class="helper-match">
    <Container>
      <div class="page-intro">
        {/*
          "Fits", not "the perfect fit". The word this site is allowed is
          the one Difference (block 04) already uses — a helper whose
          experience fits what you described. A promise of a perfect match
          is banned outright by tests/pages.test.ts, and for a reason
          stronger than the guard: nobody can promise it, and a family that
          has had a placement go wrong is exactly who reads this page.
        */}
        <h1>Finding a Helper Who Fits Your Household</h1>
        <p class="page-lede">
          Matching here is a conversation, not a catalogue. You tell us about your household; our
          team recommends a small number of helpers whose experience fits what you described, and
          you interview them yourself.
        </p>
      </div>

      {/*
        This <h2> is structural as well as editorial: the two panels below
        set their titles as <h3>, so without it the document would go <h1>
        straight to <h3> and break its own outline — the same skipped level
        /pricing needed a heading for. tests/pages.test.ts asserts it,
        because axe's heading-order rule is a best-practice rule and sits
        outside the wcag2a/wcag2aa tags scripts/run-axe.mjs filters to.
      */}
      <h2 class="section-title">What a match is made from</h2>

      <div class="match-inputs">
        {
          matchInputs.map((input) => (
            <div class="match-input">
              <h3>{input.title}</h3>
              <p>{input.body}</p>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>

      {/*
        The sentence that has to be on this page and nowhere else.

        Its URL is /find-your-helper, and the visitor's learned expectation
        of that phrase is a grid of photographs with filters above it. Left
        unsaid, the absence of one reads as a page that has not been built
        yet. Said plainly, it is the product: the recommendation comes
        after the household is understood, which is the ONE ordering claim
        this whole page makes and which Process directly below demonstrates
        step by step.
      */}
      <p class="match-order">
        Because a match is made from both halves, there is no list of helpers to browse here. The
        recommendation comes after your household is understood — not before.
      </p>

      {/*
        The internal linking triangle (spec §4), from its new vertex.
        /pricing exists and resolves; /faq is enumerated in
        tests/links.test.ts's DEFERRED_ROUTES until its own task ships it.
        No figure is named here on purpose — a total typed into this
        sentence would be a second copy of a number that lives in
        src/data/pricing.ts, which is precisely where a stale price hides.

        "MANY OF the questions", not "the questions". The definite article
        made this a completeness claim — THE questions families ask, all of
        them, answered — over a collection of fourteen FAQ entries that
        nobody has checked against what families actually ask. This branch
        has already deleted four completeness claims and three "what
        families ask" claims for the same reason; two words close the last
        one, and the sentence still does its job of pointing at the FAQ.
      */}
      <p class="match-links">
        What a placement costs is itemised on the <a href="/pricing">pricing page</a>, and many of
        the questions families put to us are answered in the <a href="/faq">FAQ</a>.
      </p>

      {/*
        Primary before secondary, both resolved from the single-sourced
        URLs, exactly as every other CTA surface on the site renders them.
        Placed here rather than only at the foot of the page: this is the
        point at which a reader has just been told what we do with what
        they send, and the next block is literally titled "what happens
        after you submit".
      */}
      <div class="match-cta">
        <Button variant="primary" href={company.requirementFormUrl}>Submit Your Requirements</Button>
        <Button variant="secondary" href={whatsappHref} external>WhatsApp Us</Button>
      </div>
    </Container>
  </section>

  {/*
    The homepage's block 05, reframed by prop and not by copy. The four
    steps stay single-sourced in Process.astro — they went from five to
    four on 2026-08-16, and that must never need doing twice (spec §3.1).
    The lede is overridden too: Process's default derives "four steps" from
    its own array, which is the right sentence to open a homepage section
    and the wrong one directly under a paragraph that has just described
    the same sequence in prose.
  */}
  <Process
    heading="What happens after you submit"
    lede="From the form landing with us to the placement itself, in the order it happens."
  />

  <section class="match-timing">
    <Container>
      <h2 class="section-title">When you will hear from us</h2>

      {/*
        THREE FIGURES, AND NOT ONE MORE.

        "within 1 business day" is DirectHired's own wording, published in
        src/content/faq/response-time.md and allowlisted by name in
        tests/pages.test.ts's duration guard. Do not reword it to "one
        business day", "24 hours" or "2 business days" — the last of those
        is a commitment DirectHired has never made, and the guard correctly
        rejects it.

        The two placement timings are spec §2.2 verbatim: approximately 2
        weeks for a helper coming to Singapore from overseas, approximately
        1 week for a transfer helper already here, both measured from
        confirmation. "Approximately" is part of the fact, not a hedge to
        be tidied away, and no third timing exists — how long the interview
        stage runs is not something DirectHired has told us, which is why
        the closing paragraph describes what that depends on instead of
        putting a number on it.
      */}
      <dl class="timing">
        <div class="timing-row">
          <dt>Our response</dt>
          <dd>
            We aim to respond within 1 business day, whether you send your requirements through the
            form or message us on WhatsApp.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="timing-row">
          <dt>A helper coming to Singapore</dt>
          <dd>Approximately 2 weeks after you confirm the helper.</dd>
        </div>
        <div class="timing-row">
          <dt>A transfer helper already here</dt>
          <dd>Approximately 1 week after you confirm the helper.</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>

      <p class="timing-note">
        Everything before that confirmation depends on your requirements and on the interviews. We
        would rather find the right person than rush you to a decision.
      </p>
    </Container>
  </section>

  <FinalCta
    heading="Tell us about your household."
    lede="Send us what your family needs and we will come back with helpers whose experience fits it."
  />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .helper-match {
    /*
     * --space-12 top, --space-section bottom — Hero's treatment, and
     * /pricing's, for the same reason both use it: this block carries the
     * page's hero, and a full --space-section (128px at ≥1280) above an
     * image-less single-column headline spends the first screen on cream.
     */
    padding-block: var(--space-12) var(--space-section);
    background: var(--color-surface);
  }

  .page-intro h1 {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 18ch;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--size-h1);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-medium);
    line-height: 1.1;
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .page-lede {
    max-width: 56ch;
    margin: var(--space-6) 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-size: var(--size-lede);
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
  }

  /* Shared by both blocks' <h2>, at --size-h3 rather than --size-h2: these
     head a sub-part of a section the <h1> has already opened, and setting
     them at section-header scale would put three competing display sizes
     on one screen. Same size and role as /pricing's `.grid-heading`. */
  .section-title {
    margin: var(--space-12) 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--size-h3);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-medium);
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  /*
   * Two panels, each opened by a hairline. The rule is --color-border
   * (decorative, ~1.3:1 — see tokens.css, it carries no text and no
   * control boundary), not --color-brand-teal: teal as a drawn line is
   * reserved for exactly two places on this site, block 07's crossbar and
   * FinalCta's mark, and spending it on routine section furniture is what
   * makes those two stop meaning anything.
   */
  .match-inputs {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-8);
    margin-top: var(--space-6);
  }

  .match-input {
    padding-top: var(--space-6);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  .match-input h3 {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-medium);
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .match-input p {
    max-width: 46ch;
    margin: var(--space-3) 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-size: var(--size-body);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
  }

  /* At lede size and in full ink, because it is the one claim on the page
     that a visitor arriving from a competitor's filter grid needs to read
     rather than skim. */
  .match-order {
    max-width: 52ch;
    margin: var(--space-12) 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-size: var(--size-lede);
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .match-links {
    max-width: 56ch;
    margin: var(--space-6) 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-size: var(--size-body);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
  }

  /* --color-accent (6.03:1 on cream), never --color-brand-teal, which
     measures 2.89:1 and carries no text anywhere on this site. */
  .match-links a {
    color: var(--color-accent);
    text-decoration: none;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-accent);
  }

  .match-links a:hover {
    color: var(--color-accent-hover);
    border-color: var(--color-accent-hover);
  }

  .match-cta {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    margin-top: var(--space-12);
  }

  .match-timing {
    padding-block: var(--space-section);
    /* White, between Process's teal wash above and FinalCta's cream below
       — the third ground in this page's sequence, and the one that keeps
       the two neighbours from meeting. Registered in
       tests/section-rhythm.test.ts; the three grounds have to be judged
       together, so do not flip this one alone. */
    background: var(--color-surface-raised);
  }

  .match-timing .section-title {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  .timing {
    max-width: 60rem;
    margin: var(--space-8) 0 0;
  }

  /*
   * A term/description pair per row, stacked on a phone and two columns
   * from --bp-tablet up, with the rule between rows rather than around
   * them. A card each would have boxed three short facts into three
   * surfaces on a white ground, which is the generic-UI-kit reading
   * Card.astro's own header warns about; a bare paragraph each would have
   * buried the figures in prose, and the figures are the reason a visitor
   * is in this block.
   */
  .timing-row {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    padding-block: var(--space-6);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  .timing-row:last-child {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  .timing dt {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-medium);
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .timing dd {
    max-width: 52ch;
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-size: var(--size-body);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
  }

  .timing-note {
    max-width: 56ch;
    margin: var(--space-8) 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-size: var(--size-body);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
  }

  /* 48em = --bp-tablet (tokens.css); the literal is repeated by hand
     because custom properties do not resolve inside a media condition. */
  @media (min-width: 48em) {
    .match-inputs {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    }

    /* The term column is fixed rather than fluid so the three descriptions
       start on one axis; `minmax(0, 1fr)` on the second track lets it
       shrink below its content's min-content width instead of pushing the
       row wider than the container. */
    .timing-row {
      grid-template-columns: 18rem minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: var(--space-8);
      align-items: baseline;
    }
  }
</style>
